# Line endings, so a Windows checkout and a Linux one produce the same tree.
#
# The two Windows files must be UTF-8 with BOM and CRLF -- PowerShell 5.1 decodes
# a BOM-less .ps1 as GBK, and cmd's parser wants CRLF.
*.ps1   text eol=crlf
*.cmd   text eol=crlf
*.sh    text eol=lf

# Everything else is LF everywhere, stated explicitly. This is not cosmetic:
# normalising to CRLF has no "safe" moment, and each file listed here would
# otherwise pick up a different ending depending on the machine's
# core.autocrlf. Measured on Windows Git 2.x with core.autocrlf=true set at the
# *system* level (Git for Windows' installer default), cloning this repo leaves
# ten tracked files modified before anyone touches them: every .md, LICENSE and
# .gitignore. The next `git commit -a` then rewrites the whole documentation in
# CRLF, and Linux users see a 200-line diff per file.
*.md       text eol=lf
.gitignore text eol=lf
.gitattributes text eol=lf
LICENSE    text eol=lf
*.py       text eol=lf
*.yml      text eol=lf
